# Paddle API key for the sandbox MCP server.
#
# The plugin wires up two MCP servers so the agent can work against both
# Paddle environments — for example, prototyping a catalog in sandbox and
# then porting it to live. They authenticate differently:
#
# +----------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+
# | MCP server     | URL                               | Authentication            |
# +----------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+
# | paddle-sandbox | https://sandbox-mcp.paddle.com/mcp| PADDLE_SANDBOX_API_KEY    |
# | paddle-live    | https://mcp.paddle.com/mcp        | OAuth (browser sign-in)   |
# +----------------+-----------------------------------+---------------------------+
#
# paddle-live authorizes with OAuth, so there is no live key to set here.
# The first time your agent uses it, your client opens a browser window to
# sign in to Paddle and approve access. Manage those connections under
# Paddle > Connectors > MCP.
#
# Generate a sandbox key at Paddle > Developer tools > Authentication:
#   - Sandbox: https://sandbox-vendors.paddle.com/authentication-v2
#
# Sandbox keys are prefixed with `pdl_sdbx_`. Grant the scopes you want the
# agent to have — for full coverage of the skills, enable product.write,
# price.write, customer.read/write, subscription.read/write, transaction.read,
# and notification.write. During development it's fine to grant all scopes.
#
# This variable must be exported in the shell that launches your editor
# (zsh / bash profile, direnv, etc.) — the plugin reads it from the
# process env, not from this file directly.

# Sandbox environment — safe for testing, the default for most development.
PADDLE_SANDBOX_API_KEY=
